Full per-seed results and degradation analysis are in Appendix~\ref{app:rho06}.

\paragraph{Scale verification.}
At 1.7B parameters (Qwen3-1.7B, same $\rho{=}0.4$ setup), adaptive $\bstar$ achieves PPL 13.27 vs.\ 13.50 for uniform ($\Delta{=}{-}0.23$), amplifying the 0.6B gap (${\sim}$0.04) by ${\sim}5.75\times$.
This comparison involves a training asymmetry: adaptive training resumed from a mid-run checkpoint with preserved optimizer state, while the uniform baseline re-initialized both optimizer and learning rate schedule.
The 0.23 PPL gap substantially exceeds typical optimizer state effects, supporting the directional conclusion that adaptive reweighting benefits grow with model scale.


\subsection{Hyperparameter Sensitivity}

\paragraph{Scale verification.}
At 1.7B parameters (Qwen3-1.7B, $\rho{=}0.4$), adaptive $\bstar$ achieves PPL 13.27 vs.\ 13.50 for uniform ($\Delta{=}{-}0.23$), ${\sim}5.75\times$ the 0.6B gap.
A training asymmetry applies (adaptive resumed with preserved optimizer state; uniform re-initialized), but the 0.23 gap far exceeds typical optimizer effects, supporting the directional finding that benefits grow with scale.

